dict(pas="13.17", w="子夏=zǐ xià/Zixia | 為=wéi/being | 莒父=jǔ fǔ/Jufu's//Ju (a place name)//fu (read fǔ in place names) | 宰，/steward | ^問/asked about | 政。/government | ^子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^無/do not | 欲/want | 速，=sù/speed; | ^無/do not | 見=jiàn/have (your) eye on | 小/small | 利。/gains | ^欲/want | 速，=sù/speed | 則/then | 不/(you) do not | 達；/get through; | ^見=jiàn/have (your) eye on | 小/small | 利，/gains | 則/then | 大/great | 事/matters | 不/are not | 成。」/brought to completion",
  l="Zixia, being steward of Jufu, asked about government.\nThe Master said: “Do not want speed;\ndo not have your eye on small gains.\nWant speed, and you do not get through;\nhave your eye on small gains, and great matters are not brought to completion.”",
  i="When Zixia was steward of Jufu he asked about governing. The Master said: “Do not try to hurry things, and do not keep looking for petty advantage. Hurry, and you will never get there; look for petty advantage, and nothing great will ever be finished.”",
  n="子夏 Zixia is the disciple Bu Shang 卜商 (1.7). 莒父 Jufu was a town of Lu (Zhu Xi: 魯邑名; Zheng Xuan in He Yan: by the old account, a lesser town of Lu); 父 is read fǔ, as in place names (Zhu Xi: 音甫). 宰 'steward', the governor of a town. 無 'do not' forbids (He Yan's text writes 毋). 見 jiàn 'to see', here 'to fix one's eye on'. 欲速則不達: each of the last two lines is a condition and its result, joined by 則 'then'. Zhu Xi: wanting things done quickly, one is hurried and without order, and fails to get through; seeing the profit in small things, what one achieves is small and what one loses great. He quotes Master Cheng: when Zizhang asked about government he was told 'dwell on it without wearying, carry it out with wholeheartedness' (12.14); when Zixia asked, this. Zizhang's fault was always to aim too high and fall short of humaneness, Zixia's to stay with the near and the small; so each was told what bore closely on himself. Kong Anguo (in He Yan): affairs cannot be done in haste — want them fast and they do not come off; small gains get in the way of great affairs."),
